lesson physics-floating-and-sinking v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=3 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Floating and sinking"
  goal "Sort things that float from things that sink, read how deep a boat sits, and predict when a loaded boat will sink."

  look {
    scene 30x27 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 0) space=1 align=start {
        tank t float=3 sunk=2
        row boat space=2 align=start {
          boat b cargo=2 side=5 sits=1 arrows=1
          note n "Each block pushes the boat a square deeper. The water pushes back up just as hard." width=10
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      Water pushes up on everything in it. When the push up is enough to hold the thing, it floats.
      When it is not, the thing sinks to the bottom. Load a boat and it sits deeper, so the water
      pushes up harder, until the water comes over the side.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.float-or-sink count=2 seed=1
    practice physics.how-many-float count=2 seed=4
    practice physics.float-count-sunk count=2 seed=2
    practice physics.boat-depth count=2 seed=3
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.boat-predict s=5 k=4 easy-s=6 easy-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.boat-carries s=6 d=1 easy-s=4 hard-d=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A floating thing sits in the surface. A loaded boat sits deeper, until the water comes over the side."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Fill a bowl and try a cork, a coin, a candle and a stone. Size is not what decides it: a large
    piece of wood floats and a small nail does not, which is the thing most worth noticing. A plastic
    tub makes the boat; load it with coins one at a time and mark the waterline each time. The last
    question asks for the most it can carry, which means stopping one before the water comes in.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the tank holds three things, the boat sits empty or with one block, and the boat with two blocks in is well clear of the water."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the tank holds seven things, the boat carries two or three blocks, and the boat that already sits two squares deep has three to spare."
  }
}
